gm-method (evaluating a spatial-index idea like Z-order/Morton/Hilbert before adopting): Morton/Z-order's value is NARROW - it only wins when the access pattern is (a) a FLAT array indexed by the curve so spatially-near data sits in nearby memory (cache-line locality on iteration), or (b) a PREFIX-RANGE proximity query (near points share a bit-prefix). It does NOT help: an O(1) hash/Map lookup (the key being a Morton int vs a 'x,y' string changes nothing for lookup), eviction that is already spatial-distance-based, or a player-centric ring that already loads NEAREST-FIRST (a radial distance sort beats Z-order's interleave for that). So when a user proposes Morton for caches/streaming, ENUMERATE each candidate's real access pattern and adopt only where it's flat-array-by-curve or prefix-query - otherwise it's a bit-interleave util + indirection for zero measured gain (cargo-cult). Witness the existing structure (is it a Map? is the iteration already distance-sorted? is it a flat array?) before claiming a win. The honest answer is often 'the current structure is already locality-optimal for its access pattern' - record THAT, don't add Morton to look sophisticated.